#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238463} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238721} {:answer/id #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239321} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239932} {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240264} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}]}
